WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
In this course, Pete LeMaster will teach you how to create a classic Android style shooting game

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.840
with Pygame and Python, completely from scratch.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.800
Pete is a control systems engineer and creator of the LeMaster Tech YouTube channel.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.560
All right, so let's do a quick project showcase and give you a little background

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.520
in description of the project before we dive into the tutorial.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.400
All right, so some of the highlights of this project are it's really a classic arcade style

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.440
feel with like scrolling enemies of varying speeds and sizes that give you progressively

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.760
more points as they get more difficult.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.560
We created three game modes where you can see how fast you can clear the entire game

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.440
in free play mode and then in accuracy mode where you have limited ammo to get as many

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.200
points as you possibly can.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.840
And lastly, there's a timed mode to see how many points you can get before your time runs out.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.280
You can also pause and resume mid run in any game mode or restart at any time.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.760
And then your high scoring is tracked and saved in an external text file

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.520
that you can reset from the menu screen if you ever want to start fresh.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.120
The static images were all created ahead of time using Canva,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.920
but you could make your own versions using anything from PowerPoint, Google Slides, Canva,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.160
Photoshop, any other software you like.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.080
You could even just download images from the internet to customize yours.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.080
I included all code and assets including images, sound effects and music,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.880
font and text files that you would need for this full build at the GitHub link in the description

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.000
of the video as well.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.600
The basic steps that we do to build the game are import and set up pi game as well as basic

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.400
game variables and then import some of our images.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.520
Then we draw the static background images onto the game.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.200
Then we draw our gun and have it rotate based on mouse position.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.920
Then we draw the enemies into the game and then we procedurally generate the enemy's starting

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.440
coordinates so we don't have to give each enemy individual code.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.000
And then we make all of the enemies move with progressive difficulty based on what tier

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.040
enemy they are.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.360
And then we check to see if our mouse click collides with targets when we shoot.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.320
And then we advance between levels once we've killed all the enemies on that screen.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.680
And then we draw the scoring and status elements onto the screen including total shots taken,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.800
how much time has elapsed, how many total points we've scored,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.120
as well as the status saying what mode we're in.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.400
And then we show how to navigate the main menu screen, the pause screen, the game over screen

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.880
and all of the code that comes with those buttons.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.440
And then we handle game over commands and conditions as well as high score tracking

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.360
including reading and writing the high scores to an external text file.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.880
And then lastly we take a look at adding some awesome background music

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.480
and sound effects to the game.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.680
All in all it takes just over two hours to build from scratch.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.680
It's around 400 lines of code.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.200
I had a ton of fun building this.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.480
I hope you do too.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.440
So without any further ado, let's get right into the tutorial.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.640
All right, let's dive right into this tutorial.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.160
I'm sure I just finished giving you guys a killer intro.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.960
So as always when you're building a pie game game,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.960
just start by importing pie game.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.080
You may need to run pip to install it if you don't already have it installed in your package.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.920
And then we're going to be doing some stuff with radial geometry.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.080
So using radians and degrees.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.880
So just import the math package right up front as well.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.880
And then go ahead and do pie game.init to sort of initialize

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.160
everything in the pie game package that we'll be using in this game.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.360
And let's start just creating some setup variables.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.600
So go ahead and create an FPS or your frame rate which will make 60.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.360
And then create a timer using pie game.time.clock.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.960
And then let's go ahead and just grab a font real quick.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.920
So any game where you're going to be writing text,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.280
which in our case we're going to be saying like the score and the time elapsed.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.200
So we know we want font.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.920
Go ahead and define a font right up front.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.920
And I'll just call it pie game.font.font.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.280
So quick note, if you don't want to grab my full assets package from the GitHub,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.440
you'll need to create a lot of things.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.880
You can do every graphics thing that I created.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.400
You can make your own in PowerPoint or Google Slides.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.280
Or you can just use like Canva, which is a nice online design service.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.880
I made all of mine in Canva Pro.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.080
I'm sure I talked about this in the intro a little bit.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.840
But essentially I did a lot of the static text design

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.680
ahead of time so we didn't have to do every piece of it in pie game.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.520
It's definitely possible to recreate almost everything we're doing purely in pie game.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.680
But it's going to speed the tutorial up a lot and make the game look a lot smoother

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.640
to kind of use a separate application to design.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.600
Because pie game is not exactly a design tool.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.320
So you're going to see a lot of these very polished screens

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.240
that are actually screens I've already made.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.480
So I'm sure I said something about that in the intro of this video.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.880
But just in case I didn't there, I covered my butt.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:59.920
So if you want to use like a free font and not go get anything from the internet,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.480
you can just use freesandsbold.ttf.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.320
It comes installed with almost any application.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.320
Anytime you install your IDE, you're usually going to be able to find this one.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.280
Now I actually went and grabbed an font from online for free.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.920
And I put it in this font folder.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.160
It's just called myfont.ttf.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.480
So if you're following along and you have a font that you want to use,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.040
or if you just want to customize the font in your game,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.920
just go ahead and point it to there.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.240
But actually this alone is not going to work because we have it nested in a few folders.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.880
So we need to point to it, which is going to be assets forward slash font,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.040
and then forward slash my font just like that.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.560
I know a lot of the people who have been on this channel for a while have

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.440
a lot of familiarity with pie game fonts,

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.280
but I like to assume there's beginners out there every time as well.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.720
So next thing we're going to do is we're going to create two constant variables

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.160
for our screen width and height and store them in width and height.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.480
And then right away we're going to use those in screen equals piegame.display.set mode.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.160
And then just in square brackets, give it a width and a height.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.360
We're going to be referring to width and a height in this project quite a lot.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.680
So it's going to be nice to have those in variables,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.840
other than just like having them hard coded

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.920
and trying to remember what we put for everything.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.280
And if you decide you want to mess with the overall dimensions

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.480
and make it a different size,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.240
you're just going to have to scale a lot of the images that I included in the GitHub.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.480
All right, so now let's create some empty holder lists for our backgrounds,

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.840
our banners, and we'll just create level equals zero.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.800
And I think we have a different colored gun for each level as well.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:45.360
Yeah, we do.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.920
We have a different colored gun for each level.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:51.280
So we will also create a list for guns here.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.200
And we're going to create some for loops to automatically populate those.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.440
You can see in each folder, I gave each levels background, banner, and gun,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.440
just a numerical number so that we can use a for loop

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.680
and just address it to that number.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.920
So let's go ahead and take a look at how to do that.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:11.620
Excuse me.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.480
All right, so what we're going to say is for I in range,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.080
and then we don't actually want it to just be three.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.640
So there's three levels total in my version of the game.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.000
You're welcome to build it out more.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.680
But we want this for loop to give us the values one, two, and three.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.880
So to do that, we need to say from one to four,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.920
because Python for loops are not end inclusive, but they are start inclusive.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.000
So this is saying for range starting at one, going until four, but not including four.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.280
So this for loop is going to do an iteration where I equals one, two, and then three.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:55.280
And for those three things, we want to append an image to all of our lists.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.480
So we're going to do pygame dot, so bgs dot append,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.760
which is the command to add an item to a Python list.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.880
And then we're going to do pygame dot image, image dot load.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.920
And then that's the py games modules built in command to load an image into your game.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:18.160
And we're going to make them formatted strings because we need to use I in

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:25.360
there. So it's going to be f and then assets forward slash bgs forward slash and then in

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.760
curly brackets so that we can use a variable, do curly brackets, I and then dot png.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.800
So this is going to go through and it's going to grab backgrounds one, two, and three without us

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.200
needing three separate lines of code.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.800
And you could say, well, we had to set up a for loop to do that.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.760
So it's only saving us one line of code right now.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.120
But we can do the exact same thing for guns and banners.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.680
So it's going to be banners dot append.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.040
And then I'm going to copy the rest of this because it's going to be the same thing.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.560
We just need to update the direction a little bit.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.720
So instead of bgs, it's going to be banners and then for guns dot append.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.680
And I'm hoping because this is all very arcade style and very like fun,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.400
kid friendly, like not offensive that YouTube's not going to care about how much

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:19.280
YouTube's not going to care about how many times, you know, we say guns and shooting in this video,

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.760
because it is not about actual violence.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.440
It is just about a fun little game.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.880
But you never know, YouTube's got interesting rules,

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.600
but I don't think they care about the master tech too much.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:31.300
All right.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.640
And so for now, let's go ahead and just leave it at that.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.280
And I think that's probably good enough for our initial startup here.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.480
So let's just go down to the main game loop now so that we can get the screen to kind of start

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.120
showing up and then we'll start filling it in with more fun and useful stuff.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.440
So we'll say run equals true.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.640
And then while run.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:56.960
Okay.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:58.720
And so this is basically a quick and easy.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.240
We create a running variable that's a boolean true false.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.760
And we say while it's true, so while run, then the first thing we want to do every loop

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.520
is going to be timer.tick at our frame rate, our FPS.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.680
And then just really quickly to get the screen kind of populating.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.960
We'll do screen.fill.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.200
And we'll just make the background black as a default.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.280
And then probably the easiest thing to do right after that is let's go ahead and put

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.480
the banner in the background of whatever level we're in.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.400
So we'll say screen.blit and then bg's.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:39.680
And then we're going to use one, two and three as the level, but their indexes in our list

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.440
are zero, one and two.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.360
So we'll say level minus one.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.840
And then we're going to put that in position zero, zero.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.360
So that's saying we want our banner, our background image.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.920
That's this, the actual like frame for the level.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:00.000
We want that to be in the top left corner for its starting position.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.520
And then we're going to do basically the exact same command for banners.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.840
So we'll do banners, but then instead of zero, zero, it's going to be zero.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.560
And then for height, we want height minus 200.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.040
So I made my banners 200 pixels high.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.440
So that's where we want those to start.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:24.800
And now we're not going to see anything unless we make this level variable a one instead of a zero.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:30.320
Because the way I'm going to have it work is if you're in the menu or you're paused or you have

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.840
the game over screen up, we're going to treat that like level zero.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:39.840
So all the important game stuff like score and time elapsed will be active only when level is

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.280
not equal to zero.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.480
So for, we are going to start on level zero, which will take you to the main menu.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.240
But for now, let's actually start on level one, just so we can see some stuff.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.000
And then right now, this is what's called an infinite while loop.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.120
So there's actually no way to exit this loop.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.360
And that'll cause your game or your computer to crash, maybe both.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.720
So we need to just create a way to get out of the infinite loop.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:09.840
So it'll be for event in pygame.event.get, just like this.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.760
And then we're going to say if event.type is equal to and then pygame.quit,

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:22.240
which is just this unique pygame command that's about the little red X that's in the top right

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.120
corner of your window, that button is just pygame.quit.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.840
And so if we get pygame.quit, then we want run to be equal to false.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:33.840
And then we just need to come outside of our for loop and do two commands.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:37.280
The first one is pygame.display.flip.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:42.560
And then outside of our while loop, it's pygame.quit.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:47.280
And this pygame.display.flip is just essentially the command that says take everything we've

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.800
told you to draw into the screen and actually put it on the screen.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.760
And then pygame.quit is what will actually close the program.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.280
So hopefully this already just these lines of code that we've set up are going to give us

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.480
the background and banner for level one when we boot it up.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.360
And there you can go.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.920
This is actually pretty cool looking already.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.400
In my opinion, I might be biased, but I think that's pretty cool.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:14.480
And if you want to see all the levels, all you have to do is change what level we start on

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:18.000
initially to two, and you'll see our like Western theme.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.760
And then three should give us our like space theme.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:22.960
Okay, so there we go.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.160
That's pretty cool.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:28.400
Why don't we take a look at drawing the gun on the screen.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.640
So the like little arcade style gun.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:33.520
And we'll do that with a function.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:39.440
So let's come down into just underneath where we blit everything else right now.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.320
And let's say if the level is greater than zero.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:42.820
Okay.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:49.360
So if level greater than zero, then we'll do this thing called draw gun.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:55.360
And then we're going to come up in between our variables and our main game loop.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.160
And we'll create the function draw gun.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.600
I'm not going to put any caps in it.

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:01.120
And I'm going to spell it right.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:02.160
It's a one to punch.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:04.160
All right.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.720
And so the gun is basically something I want to have almost like an arcade gun.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.680
It's like rotating about the center roughly.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:13.600
And it's just really more for effect.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.840
Like the actual shooting is going to be based on your mouse, not based on this gun.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.360
This is purely for style.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.320
But I think it's really important since we're making like a arcade style game to do this step.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:27.040
So what we're going to do is we're going to say the mouse position

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.800
is equal to pi game dot mouse dot get pause.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:35.760
And I happen to think this code we're about to do is actually really cool.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.160
We're going to track where the mouse is and we're going to rotate the gun so that it's always

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:41.520
looking at the mouse.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.280
And we're going to do it with geometry, which maybe you're a computer scientist

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:48.800
because you didn't like geometry and math and you didn't want to go into physics.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:52.080
But I promise this is not going to be too bad.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:52.720
Okay.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:58.240
So the point that we want the gun to essentially pivot around will be the middle of the screen.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.400
And then kind of right at the top of the banner.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.360
Because if you think of yourself as like the player of the game,

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:07.520
you're kind of standing at the banner more or less and shooting from there.

